* t49load.a -- CONF68K test t49: `load` puts a module in the module directory.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): "load loads one or more modules specified by <path>* into memory ... The names of the modules are added to the module directory."* (Using Professional OS-9 v2.4, The OS-9 Utilities, load.)** This is the one test here that measures a UTILITY rather than the kernel* directly, and it is worth being plain about what that buys. The kernel end* of it -- F$Load registering a module, and F$Exit leaving alone anything but* the primary module -- is what makes `load` possible at all; a system that* unlinked a loaded module when the loader exited would fail this test and* would be right to, because `load` would then be a no-op on it. So the claim* is checkable without knowing which `load` is installed.** THE TARGET IS tally, AND IT MUST NOT ALREADY BE RESIDENT.** The whole assertion is "it was not in the module directory, we ran load,* now it is". If the target were already resident the second half would be* true on any system whatsoever, including one where `load` did nothing at* all -- so the test probes first and reports ERROR rather than a verdict if* the ground is not what it needs. tally is chosen because runall runs it* LAST, after every test, and F$Exit auto-unlinks a program's own primary* module: nothing else in a run leaves it resident.** WHY A MISSING `load` IS A SKIP.** `load` is not part of this suite. It lives in the repository's* test/68k-utils, has to be built before it exists at all, and is installed* into CMDS only by a harness that found it built. A disk carrying only* CONF68K therefore has nothing to fork, and the honest report is SKIP with* the code that said so -- exactly as the locking tests report SKIP on a* device with no locks. A FAIL there would be a statement about a system* that was never asked the question.** The fork is by BARE NAME for that reason: F$Fork resolves it against the* execution directory, which runall has already pointed at this suite's* CMDS, so "installed" and "findable" are the same condition and there is no* second way for the test to be wrong about where it looked.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1FLink set $00FUnLink set $02FFork set $03FWait set $04** Size of the parameter handed to load: the target name and the carriage* return the shell appends to a real command line.PARMLEN set 6** F$Wait is documented to return early on a signal, with d0.w zero because* no child died. Retrying that forever would turn a stray signal into the* one failure a conformance test must never have, so the reap is bounded --* the same rule, and the same bound, fork.i states for its own wait.WAITMAX set 20** Neither of these is an OS-9 error code, and both stay clear of the 901/902* observation band fork.i already spends, so a reader of a report never has* to wonder which kind of number they are looking at.** NOWAIT: F$Wait kept waking us for somebody else's child.NOWAIT set 900* ALREADY: the target was resident before the load, so there was nothing the* load could be shown to have done.ALREADY set 903*EXPECT set 0* psect t49load,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart* Precondition: the target must not already be in the module directory. lea target(pc),a0 moveq #0,d0 trap #0 dc.w FLink bcs.s t49absent* It was. Give back the link this probe just took -- F$Link returned the* header in a2 -- and report ERROR: the test could not set up the situation* it exists to check, which is not the same as the system getting it wrong. trap #0 dc.w FUnLink move.l #ALREADY,d6 bra.w iserror*t49absent moveq #0,d0 moveq #0,d1 moveq #PARMLEN,d2 moveq #3,d3 moveq #0,d4 lea parm(pc),a1 lea loadnm(pc),a0 trap #0 dc.w FFork bcs.w t49noload move.w d0,d5* moveq #WAITMAX,d4t49wait trap #0 dc.w FWait bcs.s t49waiterr cmp.w d5,d0 beq.s t49reaped subq.w #1,d4 bne.s t49wait move.l #NOWAIT,d6 bra.w iserrort49waiterr moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6 bra.w iserror*t49reaped* d1.w is load's exit status, and this load exits with the OS-9 error code of* the last file it could not load. A nonzero status is already the answer --* the utility itself says it failed -- so it becomes the observation without* the module directory being asked anything. Reporting it, rather than going* on to look, is what keeps "load failed and said so" from being read as* "load succeeded and the module directory lost the module". moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6 bne.w t49judge** It reports success. The claim is about the module directory, so ask it. lea target(pc),a0 moveq #0,d0 trap #0 dc.w FLink bcs.s t49missing* Resident. Drop both links before reporting -- the one this probe just took* and the one load left behind, which is the link that was holding the module* resident in the first place -- so the count returns to zero and the run is* left as it was found. Every other test here cleans up its scratch files;* this is the same obligation, spent on the module directory. trap #0 dc.w FUnLink trap #0 dc.w FUnLink moveq #0,d6 bra.s t49judget49missing moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6t49judge cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.s ispass bra.s isfail*t49noload moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6 lea skipstr(pc),a5 bra.w report* use verdict.i use report.i*loadnm dc.b "load",0target dc.b "tally",0parm dc.b "tally",13idstr dc.b "RESULT t49 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0skipstr dc.b "SKIP",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  load makes a module resident in the module directory",0 ends